1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,088
Right, well, for our presentation shall I start with the early life of Thor

2
00:00:06,088 --> 00:00:06,610
Heyerdahl?

3
00:00:06,610 --> 00:00:07,153
Sure.

4
00:00:07,153 --> 00:00:11,789
Why don't you begin with describing the type of boy he was, especially his

5
00:00:11,789 --> 00:00:13,170
passion for collecting things.

6
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:16,009
That's right, he had his own little museum.

7
00:00:16,009 --> 00:00:20,266
And I think it's unusual for children to develop their own values

8
00:00:20,266 --> 00:00:22,750
and not join in their parents' hobbies.

9
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:27,640
I'm thinking of how Heyerdahl wouldn't go hunting with his dad, for example.

10
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:31,588
Yeah, he preferred to learn about nature by listening to his mother read to

11
00:00:31,588 --> 00:00:31,870
him.

12
00:00:31,870 --> 00:00:36,621
And quite early on he knew he wanted to become an explorer when he

13
00:00:36,621 --> 00:00:37,300
grew up.

14
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:42,040
That came from his camping trips he went on in Norway I think ...

15
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:46,000
No, it was climbing that he spent his time on as a young man.

16
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:46,893
Oh, right ...

17
00:00:46,893 --> 00:00:53,145
After university he married a classmate and together, they decided to experience living on

18
00:00:53,145 --> 00:00:58,950
a small island, to find out how harsh weather conditions shaped peopled lifestyles.

19
00:00:58,950 --> 00:01:04,698
As part of their preparation before they left home, they learnt basic survival skills

20
00:01:04,698 --> 00:01:06,340
like building a shelter.

21
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:11,010
I guess they needed that knowledge in order to live wild in a remote

22
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:14,012
location with few inhabitants, cut off by the sea,

23
00:01:14,012 --> 00:01:16,680
which is what they were aiming to do.

24
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:21,801
An important part of your talk should be the radical theory Heyerdahl formed from

25
00:01:21,801 --> 00:01:26,190
examining mysterious ancient carvings that he happened to find on the island.

26
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:30,232
I think you should finish with that.

27
00:01:30,232 --> 00:01:30,810
-- OK.

28
00:01:30,810 --> 00:01:32,113
All right, Victor,

29
00:01:32,113 --> 00:01:38,194
so after your part l'll talk about Thor Heyerdahl's adult life, continuing from the

30
00:01:38,194 --> 00:01:40,800
theory he had about Polynesian migration.

31
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:46,175
Up until that time of course, academics had believed that humans first migrated to

32
00:01:46,175 --> 00:01:49,630
the islands in Polynesia from Asia, in the west.

33
00:01:49,630 --> 00:01:53,334
Yes, they thought that travel from the east was impossible,

34
00:01:53,334 --> 00:01:58,148
because of the huge, empty stretch of ocean that lies between the islands

35
00:01:58,148 --> 00:02:00,000
and the nearest inhabited land.

36
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:00,385
Yes,

37
00:02:00,385 --> 00:02:04,238
but Heyerdahl spent ages studying the cloud movements, ocean currents

38
00:02:04,238 --> 00:02:08,090
and wind patterns to find if it was actually possible.

39
00:02:08,090 --> 00:02:12,843
And another argument was that there was no tradition of large ship-building in the

40
00:02:12,843 --> 00:02:15,220
communities lying to the east of Polynesia.

41
00:02:15,220 --> 00:02:20,550
But Heyerdahl knew they made lots of coastal voyages in locally built canoes.

42
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:20,924
Yes,

43
00:02:20,924 --> 00:02:26,166
or sailing on rafts, as was shown by the long voyage that Heyerdahl did

44
00:02:26,166 --> 00:02:26,540
next.

45
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:30,972
It was an incredibly risky journey to undertake - sometimes I wonder if he

46
00:02:30,972 --> 00:02:33,700
did that trip for private reasons, you know?

47
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:37,165
To show others that he could have spectacular adventures.

48
00:02:37,165 --> 00:02:39,090
What do you think, Olivia?

49
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:43,625
Well, I think it was more a matter of simply trying out his idea,

50
00:02:43,625 --> 00:02:46,540
to see if migration from the east was possible.

51
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:46,853
Yes,

52
00:02:46,853 --> 00:02:47,791
that's probably it.

53
00:02:47,791 --> 00:02:50,918
And the poor guy suffered a bit at that time

54
00:02:50,918 --> 00:02:54,670
because the war forced him to stop his work for some years ...

55
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:54,998
Yes.

56
00:02:54,998 --> 00:02:59,592
When he got started again and planned his epic voyage, do you think it

57
00:02:59,592 --> 00:03:03,530
was important to him that he achieve it before anyone else did?

58
00:03:03,530 --> 00:03:07,365
Um, I haven't read anywhere that that was his motivation.

59
00:03:07,365 --> 00:03:12,735
The most important factor seems to have been that he use only ancient techniques

60
00:03:12,735 --> 00:03:15,420
and local materials to build his raft.

61
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:15,867
Yes.

62
00:03:15,867 --> 00:03:18,550
I wonder how fast it went.

63
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:24,500
Well, it took them 97 days from South America to the Pacific Islands.

64
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:24,853
Mm.

65
00:03:24,853 --> 00:03:28,378
And after that, Heyerdahl went to Easter Island, didn't he?

66
00:03:28,378 --> 00:03:31,199
We should mention the purpose of that trip.

67
00:03:31,199 --> 00:03:35,430
I think he sailed there in a boat made out of reeds.

68
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:35,780
No,

69
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:38,230
that was later on in Egypt, Olivia.

70
00:03:38,230 --> 00:03:38,930
-- Oh, yes,

71
00:03:38,930 --> 00:03:39,630
that's right.

72
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:44,833
But what he wanted to do was talk to the local people about their old stone carvings

73
00:03:44,833 --> 00:03:48,200
and then make one himself to learn more about the process.

74
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:48,943
I see.

75
00:03:48,943 --> 00:03:50,800
Well, what a great life.

76
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:56,373
Even though many of his theories have been disproven,he certainly left a lasting impression

77
00:03:56,373 --> 00:03:58,230
on many disciplines, didn't he?

78
00:03:58,230 --> 00:04:04,381
To my mind, he was the first person to establish what modern academics call

79
00:04:04,381 --> 00:04:05,260
practical archaeology.

80
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:05,955
I mean,

81
00:04:05,955 --> 00:04:10,818
that they try to recreate something from the past today, like he did with

82
00:04:10,818 --> 00:04:11,860
his raft trip.

83
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:19,000
It's unfortunate that his ideas about where Polynesians originated from have been completely discredited.

84
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:19,449
Yes.

85
00:04:19,449 --> 00:04:25,730
Right, well, I'll prepare a PowerPoint slide at the end that acknowledges our sources.

86
00:04:25,730 --> 00:04:30,360
I mainly used The Life and Work of Thor Heyerdahl by William Oliver.

87
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:35,807
I thought the research methods he used were very sound, although I must say

88
00:04:35,807 --> 00:04:38,530
I found the overall tone somewhat old-fashioned.

89
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:41,540
I think they need to do a new, revised edition.

90
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:42,832
Yeah, I agree.

91
00:04:42,832 --> 00:04:47,140
What about the subject matter - I found it really challenging!

92
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:49,610
Well, it's a complex issue ...

93
00:04:49,610 --> 00:04:52,535
I thought the book had lots of good points.

94
00:04:52,535 --> 00:04:54,160
What did you think of...
